  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM EDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nHigh pressure over Lake Huron is drifting east in response to an \nincoming upper-level trough. Plenty of sunshine this afternoon with \nhighs in the 60s thanks to east-southeasterly flow from the departing \nhigh pressure. Tonight, clouds from the aformentioned trough will \ngenerally pass north of the area, but perhaps some high clouds \ndrifting across the southern Michigan state line. The trough axis \npasses by Sunday morning with the trailing surface cold front to \nfollow. This results in a 8 to 10 degree temperature difference in \nthe afternoon due to northwesterly flow developing off Lake \nMichigan. Downwind locations will see highs only in the 60s, while \nthose along and south of US 24 will see mid-70s. An absence of deep \nmoisture yields a rain-free frontal passage. \n\nDuring this tranquil period of weather, our primary talking point \nremains Tuesday morning low temperatures in the 30s as high pressure \nbecomes centered overhead. Frost remains a possibility, especially \nalong the southern Michigan state line. Otherwise, warming \nthereafter with high pressure moving east once more and rising upper-\nlevel heights and dry conditions.",
